hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're talking about two  different reactions dehydration  synthesis reactions and hydrolysis  reactions that are really important in  the biochemistry of a living cell before  we can talk about what those two  reactions do first we need to understand  a couple of terms first  what are monomers well monomers are  small simple molecules that are linked  together to form larger molecules these  larger molecules are known as polymers  so monomers are used to build polymers  large polymers have another name called  macromolecules  in living cells there are four types of  essential macromolecules these are  proteins lipids carbohydrates and  nucleic acids nucleic acids include both  DNA and RNA and these all play different  roles in living cells they are also  these polymers that are made up of  different types of monomers for example  proteins are made of amino acids lipids  are made of glycerol and fatty acids  carbohydrates are made from simple  sugars also called monosaccharides and  nucleic acids are made of nucleotides so  those are the different types of  monomers that are linked together to  form these polymers now this leads us to  the question how are these monomers  assembled into polymers well it is  through the dehydration synthesis  reaction  dehydration synthesis reactions look  like the one I've drawn here this and  this are generic monomers of some sort  that need to be linked together they are  linked together by a bond being formed  between the two monomers and critically  another product of the dehydration  synthesis reaction is water and that is  where the reaction gets its name not  only are you synthesizing something  synthesis you are also essentially  dehydrating the reagents dehydrating  meaning a water is being removed and  that is where the dehydration comes from  specifically you've got this hydrogen  this hydrogen and this oxygen that are  lost to form this water molecule now the  fact that a water molecule is being lost  or removed from these reactants is where  a dehydration synthesis reaction gets  its other name sometimes you hear this  kind of reaction referred to as a  condensation reaction and that's again  because of the water molecule that is  being produced now another question how  can the polymers be broken down again  into monomers if you want the polymer to  be broken down again it is through a  hydrolysis reaction  and think about what this word means  hydrolysis it's made up of hydro a  prefix that means water and lysis which  means to break so literally this means  water breaks what is water breaking well  the water molecule is breaking the bond  between monomers breaking them down  again into the two monomeric forms  rather than being in the dimer of course  dimer means two two monomers the  important thing with the dehydration  synthesis reaction is that it can be run  again and again and again and again with  more monomers being added to the end  each time so that you can get hundreds  and hundreds of monomers all linked in a  chain now one more thing to think about  we can understand why a cell will want  to use dehydration synthesis to build  polymers because of course the cell will  need to make proteins for various  functions in the cell make lipids for  the plasma membrane make carbohydrates  for energy storage make nucleic acids  for the storage and transfer of genetic  information and dehydration synthesis is  used to build all of these types of  macromolecules but why does the cell  need the reverse well sometimes it's  really critical to be able to break  these polymers down for example when a  cell no longer needs a certain protein  including a lot of enzymes to be  functional it will need to break them  down to keep them from acting on certain  things in the cell carbohydrates as well  are important energy storage molecules  those sugars are linked together and  stored in the cell but when the cell  needs  energy it wants to break those  carbohydrates down into the individual  sugars again and so any kind of process  that requires these things to be broken  down another example is with things like  mRNA mrna is carrying genetic  information from DNA to ribosomes to be  translated into protein  and when you no longer need those  proteins you need to degrade the mRNA  that's being made and that is why  hydrolysis reactions are really just as  important in a cell as the dehydration  synthesis reactions so that is it for  today on biology professor I hope you  learned a lot and thanks for watching
 

